															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/szquirrel" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1793983); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1793983&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607935778" alt="szquirrel" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1793983)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/szquirrel" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1793983); return false;" >szquirrel</a></span> <span class="odate time_1393611984 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">28 Feb 2014 18:26</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1980623">
									

<p>As I said in the draft thread, it leaves me asking what's the point. The effect is sensory deprivation until you go crazy to death. Is it just a torture device? If so, why does anybody come out at all? Did whatever helped build it have any motivation other than to make the hoo-mans go crazy?</p>
<p>Those phobias read like a shopping list of crazy that you took down to the crazy store. Honey, they're all out of astraphobia, should we get acousticophobia instead? Also the phrases &quot;mental trauma&quot;, &quot;breaks down rational thought structures&quot;, &quot;cognitive shutdown&quot;, &quot;psychological breakdown&quot;, and &quot;violent insanity&quot; are all just non-clinical synonyms for crazy. Okay, they go crazy, I understand. It would be more horrifying to read a description of specific symptoms than just a grab-bag of words all meaning crazy.</p>
<p>Good idea, good picture, but ultimately didn't take me anywhere.</p>

							</div>

								
					
