					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/mortte" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1747237); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1747237&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607935778" alt="Mortte" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1747237)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/mortte" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1747237); return false;" >Mortte</a></span> <span class="odate time_1393675079 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">01 Mar 2014 11:57</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1981195">
									

<p>Like Pig_catapult said, the critical aspect of the cell is not that it makes you crazy (even though I put a fair amount of words into saying exactly that) but how it does so. There's no compulsion or insanity ray, it's just the inevitable outcome of a lifetime's worth of total isolation.</p>
<p>I found it very difficult to convey the experience of absence and void in clinical language, which is why I tried my best to use the interview to nudge the reader to really consider what being a disembodied consciousness would be like. The list of phobias is just meant to emphasize that subjects were left terrified of basic stimuli <em>because they could no longer rationally process the sensations.</em></p>
<p>Regardless, thank you for taking the time to critique the work. I hope it at least didn't feel like a waste of your time.</p>

							</div>

								
					
