					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/szquirrel" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1793983); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1793983&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607935778" alt="szquirrel" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1793983)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/szquirrel" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1793983); return false;" >szquirrel</a></span> <span class="odate time_1393693982 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">01 Mar 2014 17:13</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1981325">
									

<p>I get what you were going for but a list of phobias doesn't make me feel anything. Instead of just naming pathologies, I would rather you describe the actual symptoms, which is both more precise and more horrifying. It's the difference between:</p>
<ul>
<li>&quot;haphephobia&quot; (does nothing for me, plus I had to Google it)</li>
<li>vs &quot;fear of being touched&quot; (more descriptive but still rather bland)</li>
<li>vs &quot;subjects begin screaming uncontrollably when medical staff attempt physical examination&quot; (ok! now I'm getting chills)</li>
</ul>
<p>You can often convey what a person is feeling by describing how they react to things. A guy who tries to claw out his eyes while screaming &quot;TURN OFF! THE LIGHTS!&quot; is probably suffering from some photophobia, no clinical language needed.</p>
<p>Thanks for writing. It's not a waste of time at all or I wouldn't bother commenting.</p>

							</div>

								
					
